  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Addressing the Immediate Needs of People Fleeing the War in Ukraine - Slovakia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Russian Federation&#x2019;s full-scale military offensive on Ukraine that commenced on 24 February 2022 has forced millions of Ukrainians and thousands of third country nationals (TCNs) to flee to the neighbouring countries. As of 28 November 2022, Slovak authorities reported more than 994,209 arrivals from Ukraine, of which 915,270 were Ukrainians. To date, 198 persons have applied for asylum on the territory of the Slovak Republic in connection with the conflict in Ukraine, and 101,426 persons have requested temporary protection, the majority of them women. 
 
The project builds upon and expands the activities launched as part of the International Organization for Migration (IOM)&#x2019;s flash appeal, taking into consideration an expected increase in the number of vulnerable refugees/TCNs arriving in Slovakia during the harsh winter months&#x2014;December 2022 through March 2023. IOM is providing timely and evidence-based lifesaving shelter and non-food item assistance and mental health and psychosocial support services. As a result, with the support of the Government of Japan, IOM Slovakia is contributing to the improvement of the living conditions of a substantial number of conflict-affected people whose lives have been profoundly impacted by war, displacement, and ensuing struggle for integration and dignity in their new life in Slovakia.</narrative>
  </description>
